eal life he has no control but  in his fantasy life he craves it the  same is true of other killers we've seen  this season like the Son of Sam killer  David Berkowitz his psychological  profile also included the desire for  control it was only after the paper  started giving him nicknames of which he  disapproved that he reached out to shift  the narrative back in his court sending  letters which refer to him as the Son of  Sam it's this reasoning that Holden  knows the callers claiming to be the  killer were fake if the killer desired  to control the narrative and build his  own mythology why call the police  instead of going straight to the press  but Holden also has several other  telling  Jersey's given to this Atlantic killer  such as him being an african-american  male in his 20s or 30s he has to be a  good talker to lure the kids in he lives  with his parents owns a dog and thinks  he's smarter than everyone else  Queens car and alibi raised serious red  flags for the agents not only are rope  gloves and dog hairs found inside but  winds alibi doesn't check out who goes  to check on a new singer at 3:00 a.m.  but since none of the victims died by  rope and there's no physical evidence to  tie him to any of the murders they have  to let him go it's not about what we  believe is a moment we can prove back in  Virginia things aren't going so well for  the tench family after was discovered  their adopted son participated in the  murder of a toddler  Nancy 10 she has slowly gone downhill in  the beginning of the season she's  cheerful and a regular mourning involves  her making breakfast for her family now  she broods smoking cigarettes and  letting Bill fend for himself when it  comes to breakfast  is there any breakfast no there's cereal  if you want at the end of the season  bill arrives home to find she's packed  up and left she can't handle the way her  son is treated in the constant back  talking of neighbors bill although he's  tried his best has spent most of his  time balancing the case in Atlanta and  his life in Virginia but it's both bill  and Nancy's feelings that they have  somehow contributed to Brian's  involvement in this murder that both of  these characters struggle with was this  inside him what do we got him bill knows  a lot about this in examining serial  killers he's seen how traumatic  childhood events usually the result of  abusive parents have shaped them ed  Kemper for example outlines how his  desire to kill women was the result of  an emotionally and physically abusive  alcoholic mother did bill and Nancy do  something that caused Brian to take part  in such a vicious act or was it in his  very DNA she isn't winning mother of the  year anytime soon back in Atlanta  Holden intent follow-up on Wayne finding  out he does in fact live with his parent  and own a dog perhaps even creepier is  the fact he sends out flyers looking for  young and new talent he's a music  manager who spends his time looking for  the next Michael Jackson while following  up at the Music Studio they find out he  rarely does any recording rather spends  most of his time just talking with the  kid and if that wasn't weird they get  their hands on a composite sketch an  eyewitness took and the resemblance is  uncanny back at the task force holding  surmises that Wayans motivation for the  killings is shame he hates himself so  much that he takes it out on these kids  he's ashamed of his failure to achieve  which is why he exaggerates his music  career and pumps himself up the agents  also learned that he was burning stuff  in his backyard at 5:00 a.m. and that  Wayne also happened to be the crime  scene photographer at one of his victims  own crime scenes they get a warrant to  search his home and not only find the  green carpet fibers from some of his  victims but also a book on how to beat a  lie detector and even though when they  interview him he can't keep his alibi  straight they still must let him go up  until this point there's no proof he did  any of it thus he has to be set free  there's a great scene after Wayne is  released in which he stops to admire the  press something Holden picks up on they  also interview a man by the name of mr.  Marcus a close friend of Wayne's who's  looking for that $100,000 reward he  tells him that one time some people came  up to them asking for donations for the  missing children and that Wayne shooed  them away seeing these kids were a bunch  of prostitutes and not worth their time  or money this sentiment that these kids  somehow deserved it for being hustlers  and giving black folks a bad name is  reiterated two other times once when  interviewed by the press and second when  interviewed by Holden and Bill Jim  Barney their man on the ground in  Atlanta follows wings dad to a nearby  airfield where he finds out he was  inquiring about tickets to South America  hinting that Wayne may be on his way out  of the country it isn't until District  Attorney Slayton is promised a  conviction from the Justice Department  that Wayne is arrested Slayton wanted  his men to make the rest in front of the  press it's politics after all which is  why Holden and Bill didn't even know it  was happening one of the things this  season does well is show how politics  and justice in the American criminal  to system go hand-in-hand take for  example how the Atlanta mayor shooed  away the FBI after he found out they may  have a black predator on the loose  something he didn't want in the  spotlight with an election looming the  case itself as Holden and Bill go on to  note has several unanswered questions  such as the missing Polaroids of young  black men which seemingly vanished from  the Atlanta PD evidence room or why some  of the men with known sexual assault  backgrounds were never investigated  Holden goes to meet the mothers of the  missing children but the arrest of Wayne  is no solace for them they know all too  well the loss of their children will be  used as a publicity stunt to further the  careers of politicians Holden vows he'll  continue to work on the case which is  quickly deflated in the next scene when  he's told by the FBI director that the  cases will soon be classified as  inactive for some justice has been  served for others it has not we also see  dr. Wendy Carr throwing out the  magazines of her now ex-girlfriend  signifying her moving on from that  relationship she's gone on her own  journey of self-discovery this season  becoming more in touch with her  sexuality and more confident in her  place at work even venturing out in  interviewing some of the most infamous  serial killers in America the season  ends just like season one on a character  known as the ADT serviceman or the BTK  killer real-life killer Dennis Rader  we've seen bits and pieces of him over  the course of the season each new  segment giving us a glimpse of who this  man is and what makes him tick in a way  these short segments are giving us the  viewer our own psychological profile of  who this man really is considering  season 1 used this same technique it's  possible they may continue this next  season as Rader wasn't even caught until  2005 if and how the show decides to  incorporate him into the main storyline  remains to be seen but it seems they  were setting him up to be the main  antagonist for a future season if you  want to know more about the BTK killer  i'll be talking about him in my  real-life killers of minehunters season  2 video that's it for this video if you  liked it make sure to LIKE and subscribe  you can also follow me on twitter at  things story YT and  Bure daddy loves you very much

